    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Collagen peptides are collagen that has been broken apart with water and enzymes
        into fragments small enough to cross the gut wall, mostly under 3,000 Daltons.</strong>
        Once absorbed, a few specific fragments reach the bloodstream intact and act as signals to
        the cells that build new collagen. Trials that showed benefit used 2.5g to 10g daily.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Native collagen cannot be absorbed.</strong> A collagen molecule is a triple helix weighing roughly 300,000 Daltons. Hydrolysis cuts it into pieces about a hundredth of that size.</li>
        <li><strong>Two dipeptides do most of the interesting work.</strong> Pro-Hyp and Hyp-Gly survive digestion, appear in blood within an hour or two, and prompt fibroblasts and chondrocytes to increase matrix production.</li>
        <li><strong>Effective doses are gram-level.</strong> Skin trials used 2.5g to 5g daily over 8 to 12 weeks. Joint trials used 5g to 10g over 12 to 24 weeks.</li>
        <li><strong>The honest limit:</strong> peptides in blood are measurable and the clinical outcomes are moderate, but nobody has proven that a swallowed peptide ends up inside a specific wrinkle or a specific knee. The mechanism is inferred, not observed.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

