      <h2 id="what-are-they">What are collagen peptides?</h2>

      <p>
        Collagen peptides are short chains of amino acids produced by breaking whole collagen into
        fragments, a process called hydrolysis. They are the same protein you already have in your
        skin, bone and cartilage, only cut into pieces small enough that your intestine can move
        them into the bloodstream. Everything else in this article follows from that one structural
        change.
      </p>

      <p>
        Start with the parent molecule. Collagen is the protein that gives connective tissue its
        tensile strength, and it accounts for roughly a third of all the protein in the human body.
        Its shape is unusual: three long polypeptide chains wound around each other into a rope,
        the triple helix, stabilised by an amino acid found almost nowhere else in nature at this
        density. That amino acid is hydroxyproline. It is going to matter later, so remember it.
      </p>

      <p>
        In tissue, those triple helices bundle into fibrils and fibrils bundle into fibres. The
        result is mechanically excellent and nutritionally useless in its raw state. You cannot eat
        a tendon and expect the collagen to arrive anywhere. The digestive tract has to dismantle
        it, and if it cannot dismantle it far enough, the protein passes through largely unused.
      </p>

      <p>
        Hydrolysis does the dismantling in advance, in a factory rather than in your gut. Collagen
        is extracted from fish skin and scales, or from bovine hide, then treated with water, heat
        and specific proteolytic enzymes that cut the chains at predictable points. What comes out
        is a fine, near-tasteless powder that dissolves in cold water without gelling. On labels
        you will see it written as hydrolysed collagen, hydrolyzed collagen with a z in American
        labelling, collagen hydrolysate, or collagen peptides. Those four terms describe the same
        material.
      </p>

      <p>
        Gelatin sits in the middle and confuses people. Gelatin is collagen that has been denatured
        by heat, so the triple helix has unwound, but the chains have not been chopped into small
        peptides. That is why gelatin sets into a jelly and why collagen peptides do not. It is
        also why a bowl of homemade bone broth is not a substitute for a measured peptide dose,
        a point I make in the OPD at least twice a week.
      </p>

