      <h2 id="daltons">Why does size matter, and what is a Dalton?</h2>

      <p>
        Size matters because the small intestine only lets small things through, and a Dalton is
        simply the unit used to weigh molecules. One Dalton is roughly the weight of a single
        hydrogen atom. Peptides below about 2,000 to 3,000 Daltons cross the intestinal wall in
        measurable quantities; a 300,000 Dalton triple helix does not cross at all.
      </p>

      <p>
        Think of the gut lining as a wall with two kinds of opening. Free amino acids and very
        short peptides, two or three units long, have dedicated transporters that carry them
        through the cells lining the intestine. Larger molecules have no such escort. They either
        get broken down further or they continue down the tract and leave.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is why the number on a peptide specification sheet, the average molecular weight, is
        the most informative figure a manufacturer can publish and the one most rarely printed on a
        consumer pack. A good hydrolysate averages somewhere between 1,000 and 3,000 Daltons, which
        works out to peptide chains of roughly ten to thirty amino acids. Push the hydrolysis too
        far and you end up with free amino acids, which is just an expensive way of buying glycine.
        Do not push far enough and you have sold your customer flavoured gelatin.
      </p>

      <p>
        There is a marketing trap here worth naming. Lower is not automatically better. Brands
        advertising extremely low molecular weights, in the low hundreds of Daltons, are describing
        a product closer to free amino acids than to the peptides used in the published trials. The
        specific fragments that seem to do the signalling are two and three amino acids long, and
        they need to arrive as fragments, not as their component parts. A number in the 1,000 to
        3,000 range is what the clinical literature actually studied.
      </p>

