      <h2 id="mechanism">How do collagen peptides actually work?</h2>

      <p>
        They work as signals, not as building material. A small number of specific fragments,
        chiefly the dipeptides Pro-Hyp and Hyp-Gly, survive digestion intact, appear in the
        bloodstream, and reach connective tissue where they appear to prompt fibroblasts and
        chondrocytes to increase their output of new collagen matrix. Your body does not take a
        swallowed peptide and slot it into your skin.
      </p>

      <p>
        That distinction is the whole article in one line. <strong>Collagen peptides are not
        bricks, they are instructions.</strong> The intuitive model, that you eat collagen and it
        becomes your collagen, is wrong in a way that actually matters, because if it were true
        then any protein with a decent glycine content would do the same job, and the trials would
        not show a difference between collagen peptides and an equivalent dose of ordinary protein.
      </p>

      <p>
        Here is the sequence as the research describes it. Hydrolysed collagen is unusually rich in
        proline and hydroxyproline. During digestion most of it is broken down to free amino acids
        like any other protein. But peptide bonds involving hydroxyproline resist the common
        digestive enzymes, so a fraction escapes complete breakdown. Iwai and colleagues showed in
        2005 that after a dose of collagen hydrolysate, human blood contains identifiable
        hydroxyproline-containing peptides, with Pro-Hyp the most abundant, peaking within one to
        two hours.
      </p>

      <p>
        Then the interesting part. Shigemura and colleagues reported in 2009 that Pro-Hyp added to
        cultured fibroblasts, the cells that manufacture collagen in skin, increased their growth
        and activity. Later work has described similar behaviour in chondrocytes, the equivalent
        cells in cartilage. The working model is that circulating Pro-Hyp acts something like a
        damage signal: the body reads a spike in collagen fragments as evidence that collagen
        somewhere has been broken down, and responds by upregulating repair. Whether that is the
        complete explanation, I would not bet on. It is the best one currently available.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two honest caveats belong here rather than at the end. First, most of the fibroblast and
        chondrocyte work is in cell culture and animal models, and cells in a dish behave more
        obligingly than cells in a fifty-year-old knee. Second, nobody has traced an individual
        ingested peptide to a specific site of new collagen deposition in a living human. We have a
        measurable blood signal, a plausible cellular mechanism, and clinical outcomes at the far
        end. The middle of the chain is inferred.
      </p>

