      <h2 id="evidence">What does the evidence show for collagen peptides at 5g to 10g?</h2>

      <p>
        Across skin and joint outcomes, the evidence is consistent, moderate in size, and slow to
        appear. Skin trials at 2.5g to 5g daily have repeatedly improved measured elasticity and
        hydration over 8 to 12 weeks. Joint trials at 5g to 10g daily have reduced activity-related
        pain over 12 to 24 weeks. Nothing in that literature is dramatic.
      </p>

      <h3>Skin: Proksch et al., 2014</h3>
      <p>
        A double-blind, placebo-controlled trial gave women either 2.5g or 5g of specific collagen
        peptides daily for eight weeks. Skin elasticity, measured instrumentally rather than by
        self-report, improved significantly against placebo in both dose groups, with the effect
        still partially present four weeks after supplementation stopped. Two things stand out: the
        lower dose worked, and the benefit faded once the signal stopped arriving. The fuller
        review of that literature sits in
        <a href="/pages/collagen-for-skin">collagen for skin</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Joints: Clark et al., 2008</h3>
      <p>
        The most cited joint trial followed 147 athletes at Penn State with activity-related joint
        pain for 24 weeks on 10g of collagen hydrolysate daily against placebo. The collagen group
        reported significantly less pain when walking, standing, carrying and lifting. It is a
        functional result rather than an imaging result, which is worth remembering: pain scores
        improved, cartilage was not shown to regrow.
      </p>

      <h3>Joints at a lower dose: Zdzieblik et al., 2017</h3>
      <p>
        Young adults with knee discomfort but no diagnosed arthritis took 5g of specific collagen
        peptides daily for 12 weeks and reported significantly reduced activity-related knee pain.
        This trial covers the largest real-world group, people with early wear rather than
        established disease. The joint evidence in full sits in
        <a href="/pages/collagen-for-joint-pain">collagen for joint pain</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Bone: König et al., 2018</h3>
      <p>
        Postmenopausal women with reduced bone mineral density took 5g of collagen peptides daily
        for twelve months and showed increased bone mineral density against placebo. Twelve months
        is the point. Bone and cartilage operate on a timescale that makes a four-week trial of any
        supplement close to meaningless.
      </p>

      <p>
        Where does that leave the sceptic's objection, that collagen peptides are just protein?
        Partly answered and partly open. Several trials used an isonitrogenous control, meaning the
        placebo group received an equivalent amount of other protein, and collagen still came out
        ahead, which points at the peptide signal rather than the amino acids. But collagen is a
        poor protein by conventional standards, missing tryptophan entirely, and it should never
        replace dal, paneer, eggs or whey in your daily intake. That comparison has its own article:
        <a href="/pages/collagen-vs-whey-protein">collagen vs whey protein</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="labels">How do you read a hydrolysed collagen label in India?</h2>

      <p>
        Look for three things: the word hydrolysed or peptides rather than plain collagen, a dose
        stated in milligrams or grams of collagen itself rather than of a proprietary blend, and an
        FSSAI licence number on the pack. If any of the three is missing, you are guessing.
      </p>

      <p>
        The vocabulary first. On an Indian label you will encounter <strong>hydrolysed collagen</strong>,
        <strong>hydrolyzed collagen peptides</strong>, <strong>collagen hydrolysate</strong> and
        <strong>collagen peptides</strong>, and for practical purposes these are interchangeable.
        What should make you pause is a label that says only "marine collagen" or "collagen
        protein" with no mention of hydrolysis, because unhydrolysed collagen in a drink is either
        badly described or badly formulated.
      </p>

      <p>
        Then the dose, which is where most of the disappointment in this category comes from. The
        trials above used 2.5g to 10g. A sachet declaring "collagen blend 1,000mg" is at best a
        tenth of a studied joint dose, and the word blend usually means the collagen is sharing
        that thousand milligrams with several other ingredients. Convert everything to grams before
        you compare, because 10,000mg and 10g are the same thing and packs switch units precisely
        because the larger number looks better.
      </p>

      <p>
        A few more checks I would apply, having read a lot of these packs:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Source and type.</strong> Marine collagen from fish is type I, bovine is type I and III. If a pack says type II at gram-level doses, the type designation is doing no useful work, since hydrolysed type II behaves like any other peptide.</li>
        <li><strong>Serving size versus per-100g figures.</strong> Some packs state content per 100g of powder, which is not what you are swallowing. Read the per-serving column.</li>
        <li><strong>Added vitamin C.</strong> Not marketing. Vitamin C is a required co-factor for the enzymes that hydroxylate proline during collagen synthesis, so a peptide product without it is asking your body to build with one tool missing.</li>
        <li><strong>Sugar.</strong> Many Indian collagen drinks carry 8g to 15g of added sugar per serving to cover the taste. Check the nutrition panel, not the front of the pack.</li>
        <li><strong>FSSAI licence.</strong> A fourteen-digit number on the label. Its absence on an imported pack sold in India is a genuine red flag.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Format is a separate question, and one that changes the answer on both cost and compliance.
        Tablets, capsules and liquid shots are compared properly in
        <a href="/pages/collagen-tablets-vs-shots">collagen tablets vs capsules vs shots</a>, so I
        will restrict myself to the arithmetic that belongs here: to reach 10g of collagen from
        500mg tablets you would swallow twenty of them a day, which is why almost no tablet product
        in the Indian market is delivering a studied dose.
      </p>

