      <h2 id="timing">Does timing matter, or only consistency?</h2>

      <p>
        Consistency matters, timing barely does. The empty-stomach rule that circulates online has
        no meaningful trial support, and the studies that produced the results everyone quotes did
        not control the time of day at all. Take it whenever you will reliably remember to take it.
      </p>

      <p>
        The empty-stomach argument runs like this: food in the stomach slows gastric emptying and
        competes for absorption, therefore peptide levels in blood will be lower. The first half is
        true and the second half does not follow in any way that has been shown to change an
        outcome. Peak plasma levels may shift by an hour. No trial has demonstrated that this
        alters skin elasticity or joint pain scores after twelve weeks, and the trials that
        produced those endpoints mostly instructed participants to take the dose daily without
        specifying conditions.
      </p>

      <p>
        The bedtime claim is similar. Growth hormone does peak in early sleep, and that is
        biologically real, but the leap from that fact to "therefore collagen must be taken at
        night" is an inference nobody has tested against a morning dose. If taking it at night helps
        you remember, take it at night for that reason and not because of a hormone chart.
      </p>

      <p>
        What does appear to matter, based on how these trials were run and on what I see in
        follow-up:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Daily, without gaps.</strong> The Pro-Hyp signal clears within hours. A dose taken four times a week is a signal that stops arriving for most of the week.</li>
        <li><strong>A full dose, not a split one.</strong> Trials gave the full 2.5g to 10g as a single daily serving. There is no evidence that splitting helps and it doubles the number of chances to forget.</li>
        <li><strong>Vitamin C on board.</strong> Either in the product or from your diet. Amla, guava, citrus and capsicum all do the job.</li>
        <li><strong>Twelve weeks before you judge it.</strong> Proksch measured skin at eight weeks; the joint and bone trials needed twelve weeks to twelve months. Stopping at three weeks tells you nothing.</li>
      </ul>

