      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>What is the difference between collagen and collagen peptides?</summary>
          <p>
            Collagen peptides are collagen that has been hydrolysed, meaning broken into fragments
            of roughly 1,000 to 3,000 Daltons. Whole collagen is a triple helix of about 300,000
            Daltons and is too large to be absorbed. The protein is identical; only the fragment
            size differs, and that difference is what makes peptides usable.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is hydrolyzed collagen the same as collagen peptides?</summary>
          <p>
            Yes. Hydrolysed collagen, hydrolyzed collagen peptides, collagen hydrolysate and
            collagen peptides all describe the same enzymatically broken-down material. The spelling
            with a z is simply American labelling convention. Gelatin is not the same thing, because
            it is unwound but not cut into small peptides.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How do collagen peptides get absorbed into the body?</summary>
          <p>
            Peptides under roughly 2,000 to 3,000 Daltons cross the intestinal wall using peptide
            transporters, and a fraction containing hydroxyproline resists digestion completely.
            Iwai and colleagues detected these fragments, mainly Pro-Hyp, in human blood peaking one
            to two hours after an oral dose.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How many grams of collagen peptides should I take daily?</summary>
          <p>
            Skin trials used 2.5g to 5g daily and joint trials used 5g to 10g daily. Skelecoll
            provides 10,000mg, which is 10g, in a single 30ml shot, matching the dose used in the
            24-week athlete trial by Clark and colleagues. Take it daily rather than in bursts.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Should collagen peptides be taken on an empty stomach?</summary>
          <p>
            It makes little practical difference. Food may shift peak blood levels by about an hour,
            but no trial has shown that this changes skin or joint outcomes at twelve weeks. Pick
            the time of day you will actually stick to, because consistency changes results and
            timing does not.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Are collagen peptides the same as collagen protein powder?</summary>
          <p>
            Usually yes, since most products sold as collagen protein are hydrolysed peptides. But
            collagen is an incomplete protein with no tryptophan, so it should not replace whey,
            dal, paneer or eggs in your protein intake. Treat it as a targeted supplement for
            connective tissue rather than a protein source.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

